{"adrs":[{"kind":"adr","id":"0001","num":1,"slug":"discover-crux-files-by-header","file":"docs/adr/0001-discover-crux-files-by-header.md","mtime":1791281136723,"title":"Discover crux files by their header, not by name or folder","subtitle":"","status":"Accepted","statusKey":"accepted","date":"2026-10-06","author":"abtin","owner":"","due":"","reopen":["header scan takes more than 1 s on a 10k-file repo"],"applies":["internal/adr/**"],"tags":["format","discovery"],"components":["graph","cli"],"rel":{},"mentions":[],"facts":{"decision":"Any .md file whose frontmatter has a crux: key joins the graph, wherever it is. The value is the kind. File names and folders carry no meaning, except that a code kind's folder is its default code: path and an enclosing container is its default parent.","question":null,"recommendation":null,"options":[{"panel":"C","title":null,"items":[{"key":"A","name":"Header key `crux:` anywhere","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"ok","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"No convention. Files live next to code."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"Must walk the repo."}]},{"key":"B","name":"Fixed folders (`docs/system/…`)","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"no","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Cheap to find."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"Far from code; a second tree to keep in sync."}]},{"key":"C","name":"One YAML map","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"no","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"One file to read."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"Grows unreadable; merge conflicts; no prose."}]}]}],"questions":[],"consequences":["Positive: files sit next to the code; code: and parents need no typing.","Cost: every load walks the repo; .gitignore, hidden and vendor folders are skipped and only the frontmatter is read.","Follow-up: architecture.yaml stays optional for hand-drawn layouts."],"criteria":[]},"sections":[{"id":"A","title":"Decision"},{"id":"B","title":"Context"},{"id":"C","title":"Options"},{"id":"D","title":"Consequences"}],"text":"Decision span=3 Any .md file whose frontmatter has a crux: key joins the graph, wherever it is. The value is the kind. File names and folders carry no meaning, except that a code kind's folder is its default code: path and an enclosing container is its default parent. Context span=3 - Teams keep docs in different places; a naming convention would fight every existing layout. - A service's description belongs next to its code, where agents and reviewers already look. - The previous model needed one architecture.yaml ; it grew large and drifted from the code. Options span=3 Option Pros Cons Fit --- --- --- --- A — Header key crux: anywhere No convention. Files live next to code. Must walk the repo. ok B — Fixed folders ( docs/system/… ) Cheap to find. Far from code; a second tree to keep in sync. no C — One YAML map One file to read. Grows unreadable; merge conflicts; no prose. no Consequences span=3 - Positive: files sit next to the code; code: and parents need no typing. - Cost: every load walks the repo; .gitignore , hidden and vendor folders are skipped and only the frontmatter is read. - Follow-up: architecture.yaml stays optional for hand-drawn layouts."},{"kind":"adr","id":"0002","num":2,"slug":"elements-and-typed-connections","file":"docs/adr/0002-elements-and-typed-connections.md","mtime":1791281136726,"title":"Elements with typed connections; contracts as transport:name","subtitle":"","status":"Accepted","statusKey":"accepted","date":"2026-10-06","author":"abtin","owner":"","due":"","reopen":[],"applies":["internal/adr/**","skills/crux/reference.md"],"tags":["format","graph"],"components":["graph"],"rel":{"depends_on":["0001"]},"mentions":[],"facts":{"decision":"Describe the system as elements of about twenty kinds (service, store, broker, contract, entity, flow, question…). Connect them with typed frontmatter lists (calls, exposes, uses, publishes, consumes, reads, writes, owns, decided_by, blocked_by…). A connection to an interface is a contract reference transport:name; a reference creates the contract, a contract file adds detail.","question":null,"recommendation":null,"options":[{"panel":"C","title":null,"items":[{"key":"A","name":"Typed lists + contract refs, derived edges","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"ok","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Each side writes its own fact. Topics connect producers and consumers automatically."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"A vocabulary to learn."}]},{"key":"B","name":"Free arrows with labels","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"no","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Easy to write."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"No protocol, no checks, no derived links."}]},{"key":"C","name":"Full C4 / Structurizr model","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"warn","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Rich and known."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"A second DSL; far from the code; no decisions or flows."}]}]}],"questions":[],"consequences":["Positive: lint can check flow hops and contract sides; the map and element pages need no extra input.","Cost: the vocabulary must stay small and stable — aliases absorb common words (worker, database, emits)."],"criteria":[]},"sections":[{"id":"A","title":"Decision"},{"id":"B","title":"Context"},{"id":"C","title":"Options"},{"id":"D","title":"Consequences"}],"text":"Decision span=3 Describe the system as elements of about twenty kinds (service, store, broker, contract, entity, flow, question…). Connect them with typed frontmatter lists ( calls , exposes , uses , publishes , consumes , reads , writes , owns , decided by , blocked by …). A connection to an interface is a contract reference transport:name ; a reference creates the contract, a contract file adds detail. Context span=3 - Agents need to answer \"how does X reach Y\" by walking hops, not by grepping. - A bare arrow loses the protocol; a separate interface file for every endpoint is too much work. - Publisher and consumer are written by different teams and should not have to name each other. Options span=3 Option Pros Cons Fit --- --- --- --- A — Typed lists + contract refs, derived edges Each side writes its own fact. Topics connect producers and consumers automatically. A vocabulary to learn. ok B — Free arrows with labels Easy to write. No protocol, no checks, no derived links. no C — Full C4 / Structurizr model Rich and known. A second DSL; far from the code; no decisions or flows. warn Consequences span=3 - Positive: lint can check flow hops and contract sides; the map and element pages need no extra input. - Cost: the vocabulary must stay small and stable — aliases absorb common words ( worker , database , emits )."},{"kind":"adr","id":"0003","num":3,"slug":"catalog-styles-tech-and-transports","file":"docs/adr/0003-catalog-styles-tech-and-transports.md","mtime":1791281136729,"title":"A built-in catalog styles technologies and transports","subtitle":"","status":"Accepted","statusKey":"accepted","date":"2026-10-06","author":"abtin","owner":"","due":"","reopen":[],"applies":["internal/catalog/**","web/**"],"tags":["visual","catalog"],"components":["catalog","app"],"rel":{},"mentions":[],"facts":{"decision":"Ship a catalog of technologies (label, category, color, icon, shape, detect rules) and transports (mode: sync, async, stream, data, trigger). Authors only name tech: and transports; boxes, chips, edge styles, icons and legends follow. Icons come from Simple Icons (CC0), bundled only for catalog entries. Projects extend it in .crux/catalog.yaml.","question":null,"recommendation":null,"options":[],"questions":[],"consequences":["Positive: one source for look and detection; consistent across map, element pages and components.","Cost: the catalog needs care as tools change; unknown tech still works with a monogram."],"criteria":[]},"sections":[{"id":"A","title":"Decision"},{"id":"B","title":"Context"},{"id":"C","title":"Consequences"}],"text":"Decision span=3 Ship a catalog of technologies (label, category, color, icon, shape, detect rules) and transports (mode: sync, async, stream, data, trigger). Authors only name tech: and transports; boxes, chips, edge styles, icons and legends follow. Icons come from Simple Icons (CC0), bundled only for catalog entries. Projects extend it in .crux/catalog.yaml . Context span=3 - People skim pictures; every diagram should look finished without styling work. - The scanner needs the same detect rules to suggest tech: from go.mod, package.json and images. Consequences span=3 - Positive: one source for look and detection; consistent across map, element pages and components. - Cost: the catalog needs care as tools change; unknown tech still works with a monogram."},{"kind":"adr","id":"0004","num":4,"slug":"questions-and-sessions-carry-the-loop","file":"docs/adr/0004-questions-and-sessions-carry-the-loop.md","mtime":1791281136732,"title":"Questions and sessions carry the design → model → build loop","subtitle":"","status":"Accepted","statusKey":"accepted","date":"2026-10-06","author":"abtin","owner":"","due":"","reopen":[],"applies":["internal/flow/**","skills/**"],"tags":["workflow","agents"],"components":["workflow"],"rel":{},"mentions":[],"facts":{"decision":"Sessions run in the user's agent harness; crux stores their results. An unknown becomes a crux: question with raised_from (a flow step or element) and blocks. Each session ends with a crux: session file listing what it produced. Agents never fill an unknown with an assumption.","question":null,"recommendation":null,"options":[],"questions":[],"consequences":["Positive: the question board, flow routes and element pages show exactly what is open and what it blocks.","Cost: agents must follow the skills; lint reports questions pointing at missing steps."],"criteria":[]},"sections":[{"id":"A","title":"Decision"},{"id":"B","title":"Context"},{"id":"C","title":"Consequences"}],"text":"Decision span=3 Sessions run in the user's agent harness; crux stores their results. An unknown becomes a crux: question with raised from (a flow step or element) and blocks . Each session ends with a crux: session file listing what it produced. Agents never fill an unknown with an assumption. Context span=3 - Design happens in conversation; without a record it must be recapped later. - Implementation agents hit known unknowns; guessing hides them until production. Consequences span=3 - Positive: the question board, flow routes and element pages show exactly what is open and what it blocks. - Cost: agents must follow the skills; lint reports questions pointing at missing steps."},{"kind":"adr","id":"0005","num":5,"slug":"code-facts-through-adapters","file":"docs/adr/0005-code-facts-through-adapters.md","mtime":1791281136735,"title":"Code facts come from language adapters; lint compares docs with code","subtitle":"","status":"Accepted","statusKey":"accepted","date":"2026-10-06","author":"abtin","owner":"","due":"","reopen":["a team needs a language without an adapter and a facts-printing command is not enough"],"applies":["internal/scan/**","internal/lint/**"],"tags":["lint","scan"],"components":["scanner","linter","cli"],"rel":{},"mentions":[],"facts":{"decision":"A language-agnostic core compares what crux files declare with facts from the code: modules and imports, HTTP routes, gRPC services, topics and queues with their side, technologies, deployables and infrastructure. Built-in adapters cover Go, TypeScript/JavaScript and infra files; any other language plugs in a command that prints the facts JSON.","question":null,"recommendation":null,"options":[{"panel":"B","title":null,"items":[{"key":"A","name":"Pattern adapters in the binary + command plug-ins","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"ok","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"No toolchain needed; fast (2.6 s on 2,600 Go files)."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"Patterns miss wrapped clients; config adds more."}]},{"key":"B","name":"Full type-checked analysis (go/packages, tsc)","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"warn","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Exact."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"Needs toolchains and builds; slow; per-language depth."}]},{"key":"C","name":"No code checks","topic":null,"verdict":{"kind":"no","label":""},"verdictHead":"Fit","fields":[{"label":"Pros","role":"pro","text":"Simple."},{"label":"Cons","role":"con","text":"Docs drift silently."}]}]}],"questions":[],"consequences":["Positive: drift shows up as lint findings with a fix line; crux scan feeds the adopt skill.","Cost: findings are heuristics; lint.ignore and lint.topics tune them per project."],"criteria":[]},"sections":[{"id":"A","title":"Decision"},{"id":"B","title":"Options"},{"id":"C","title":"Consequences"}],"text":"Decision span=3 A language-agnostic core compares what crux files declare with facts from the code: modules and imports, HTTP routes, gRPC services, topics and queues with their side, technologies, deployables and infrastructure. Built-in adapters cover Go, TypeScript/JavaScript and infra files; any other language plugs in a command that prints the facts JSON. Options span=3 Option Pros Cons Fit --- --- --- --- A — Pattern adapters in the binary + command plug-ins No toolchain needed; fast (2.6 s on 2,600 Go files). Patterns miss wrapped clients; config adds more. ok B — Full type-checked analysis (go/packages, tsc) Exact. Needs toolchains and builds; slow; per-language depth. warn C — No code checks Simple. Docs drift silently. no Consequences span=3 - Positive: drift shows up as lint findings with a fix line; crux scan feeds the adopt skill. - Cost: findings are heuristics; lint.ignore and lint.topics tune them per project."}],"architecture":{"title":"crux","desc":"decisi